# ── Why there is no rule 404-ing missing assets ────────────────────────────────
#
# There was one, and it does not work here. Cloudflare Pages — which is what serves this site, per
# the cf-cache-status headers and the cloudflareinsights beacon — only honours 301, 302, 303, 307,
# 308 and 200 in _redirects. A line ending in 404 is dropped at build time, silently, so
#
#     /assets/*  /assets/:splat  404
#
# looked like protection and delivered none. Verified against production: a request for an asset
# that does not exist still answers 200 with Content-Type: text/html, which is what makes the
# browser say "Expected a JavaScript-or-Wasm module script".
#
# So the catch-all above will keep turning a missing chunk into HTML, and the protection has to be
# on the client, where it provably runs:
#
#   index.html                     inline handler for a missing ENTRY bundle
#   src/utils/recoverFromStaleBuild.js   Vite's vite:preloadError, for a missing LAZY chunk
#
# Both reload once to re-read this HTML, which public/_headers keeps un-cached so it always names
# the current build. Fixing it at the edge instead would mean routing /assets/* through a Pages
# Function — a Worker invocation on every asset request, to improve an error message.
